4. Relieving one constraint reveals the next

Suppose qualified HBM rises to 600,000 stacks per month. Memory can now support 100,000 accelerators, but packaging still limits production to 90,000. Site readiness still limits deployment to 70,000.

A real analysis also needs lead times, inventory, product compatibility and the ramp profile. Capacity may not be interchangeable between products. The minimum-capacity calculation is a first screening tool, not a complete forecast.

That is why an announced capacity expansion must be translated into the same product, time period and good-output units before comparison.

5. Installed hardware is only the starting point

Even after deployment, twice the advertised compute does not guarantee twice the useful output. NVIDIA's performance guide distinguishes limits from math throughput, memory bandwidth and latency; the limiting factor depends on the work being executed.

For a simple illustration, assume 70,000 identical operational accelerators, 720 hours in a 30-day month and a measured useful-throughput factor of 50% against a fixed workload baseline. That gives 25.2 million baseline-equivalent accelerator-hours.

At 60%, the same fleet gives 30.24 million: a 20% increase without another chip. This is an illustrative throughput normalization, not a standard GPU-utilization metric. Output quality and workload must stay comparable.

6. What this changes in your analysis

When someone says a component is essential, ask a second question: is more of it the thing that changes output now, and when might that change?

A supplier can have an extraordinary technology while another part of the system limits near-term growth. Conversely, an unglamorous component can have immediate operational importance. Neither observation, on its own, tells us the supplier's eventual profits.

Over the next lessons we will connect packaging, memory, chiplets and test. Later blocks cover accelerator architecture, networking, power and cooling, model workloads, and chip design. We will return to fabrication with those connections in place.

Quick check — answer before reading on

In the example, HBM improves to support 100,000 accelerators and site deployment rises to 85,000. Logic remains at 120,000 and packaging at 90,000. What limits manufacturing and what limits deployment?

Answer: packaging caps manufacturing at 90,000; site readiness caps deployment at 85,000. Calling HBM the bottleneck because it was yesterday's constraint would miss the change.

Remember these three things

- Manufacturing, deployment and runtime are different constraints.

- Compare capacities using the same product, time period and good-output units.

- Expertise means predicting how a change travels through the system and checking the prediction against evidence.

